{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "14-intervals.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "The interval lattice",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Represent partial knowledge of a number as an interval, merge intervals by intersecting them, propagate bounds through arithmetic, and see where termination needs care.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$x$ is between $1$ and $5$, $y$ between $2$ and $3$, and $x + y$ is between $0$ and $6$. What do you know about $x$?\n\n> [!try]\n> Work it out by hand before reading on.\n\nTwo steps do it. $x + y \\ge 1 + 2 = 3$, so $x + y$ is between $3$ and $6$; then $x = (x + y) - y \\le 6 - 2 = 4$. No number was ever known exactly, only bounds, and each step narrowed one. \"The value is between 1 and 5\" is partial information: more than \"unknown\", less than \"it is 3\". Two such facts about one number merge into their **intersection**, and an empty intersection is a contradiction. Ordered by information, smaller intervals are higher.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "On the integers $0$ to $2$ (write `i01` for $[0, 1]$):",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let I = poset({i02, i01, i12, i00, i11, i22, empty}; i02 < i01, i02 < i12, i01 < i00, i01 < i11, i12 < i11, i12 < i22, i00 < empty, i11 < empty, i22 < empty)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "lattice(I)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(I, i01, i12)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(I, i00, i22)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The meet goes the other way: what two facts have in common, the smallest interval containing both.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "meet(I, i00, i22)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- What is known about a number: it lies between lo and hi. -/\nstructure Ival where\n  lo : Int\n  hi : Int\n  deriving Repr, DecidableEq\n\ninstance : ToString Ival := ⟨fun a => if a.hi < a.lo then \"empty\" else s!\"[{a.lo}, {a.hi}]\"⟩\n\n/-- Merging two facts about one number: both hold, so it lies in the intersection. -/\ndef Ival.merge (a b : Ival) : Ival := ⟨max a.lo b.lo, min a.hi b.hi⟩\n\ninstance : Semilattice Ival where\n  merge := Ival.merge\n  merge_comm a b := by simp only [Ival.merge, Ival.mk.injEq]; omega\n  merge_assoc a b c := by simp only [Ival.merge, Ival.mk.injEq]; omega\n  merge_idem a := by simp only [Ival.merge]; cases a; simp only [Ival.mk.injEq]; omega\n\ndef Ival.add (a b : Ival) : Ival := ⟨a.lo + b.lo, a.hi + b.hi⟩\ndef Ival.sub (a b : Ival) : Ival := ⟨a.lo - b.hi, a.hi - b.lo⟩\n\n/-- a says at least as much as b: it is inside it. -/\ndef Ival.within (a b : Ival) : Prop := b.lo ≤ a.lo ∧ a.hi ≤ b.hi",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The same three propagators as for $x + y = z$, now on intervals. Bounds flow in every direction until they stop narrowing:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def ivalSum (x y z : Cell Ival) : List Propagator :=\n  [ do z.write (Ival.add (← x.read) (← y.read)),\n    do y.write (Ival.sub (← z.read) (← x.read)),\n    do x.write (Ival.sub (← z.read) (← y.read)) ]\n\ndef ivalExample : IO Unit := do\n  let x ← Cell.new (⟨1, 5⟩ : Ival)\n  let y ← Cell.new (⟨2, 3⟩ : Ival)\n  let z ← Cell.new (⟨0, 6⟩ : Ival)\n  let rounds ← runToFixpoint (ivalSum x y z)\n  IO.println s!\"x ∈ {← x.read}, y ∈ {← y.read}, z ∈ {← z.read}  ({rounds} rounds)\"\n\n#eval ivalExample",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The network repeats the reasoning above: $z$ narrows to $[3, 6]$, then $x$ to $[1, 4]$, and $y$ stays $[2, 3]$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem Ival.add_mono (a a' b b' : Ival) (ha : a.within a') (hb : b.within b') :\n    (Ival.add a b).within (Ival.add a' b') := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that interval addition is monotone: narrower summands give a narrower sum.",
      "leanSolution": "  simp only [Ival.within, Ival.add] at *\n  omega",
      "hints": [
        "Unfold `Ival.within` and `Ival.add` everywhere with `simp only`; what is left is linear arithmetic."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "When a contradiction never settles",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 13's loop stopped because the flat lattice has no long chains. `Ival` has infinitely many ways to be empty: `⟨5, 3⟩` and `⟨6, 3⟩` are different values that both mean \"no number\", and the second is above the first. Give the network a contradiction, $x = 5$ and $y = 5$ but $x + y = 0$, and watch $x$'s raw bounds round by round:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- x = 5 and y = 5, but x + y = 0: four rounds of the propagators, and x's bounds after each. -/\ndef ivalContradiction : IO Unit := do\n  let x ← Cell.new (⟨5, 5⟩ : Ival)\n  let y ← Cell.new (⟨5, 5⟩ : Ival)\n  let z ← Cell.new (⟨0, 0⟩ : Ival)\n  for round in [1, 2, 3, 4] do\n    for p in ivalSum x y z do\n      let _ ← p\n    let v ← x.read\n    IO.println s!\"round {round}: x = ⟨{v.lo}, {v.hi}⟩, which is {v}\"\n\n#eval ivalContradiction",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> Every round changes the cells, so `runToFixpoint` on this network would never return, although every cell has meant \"empty\" since the first round. The fix is to make all empty intervals one element, a single top, as `Flat` has a single `conflict`. Then a cell that starts with finite bounds can narrow only finitely often, and the loop stops. Termination comes from the lattice, so a lattice with infinite ascending chains needs this care, or the *widening* operators of static analysis, which jump ahead to a bound instead of creeping towards it.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(I, i02, i12)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Merge $[0, 2]$ with $[1, 2]$. Answer with the element's name.",
      "hints": [
        "Intersect them."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(I, i01, i22)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Merge $[0, 1]$ with $[2, 2]$.",
      "hints": [
        "Do they overlap?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Intervals are partial information about numbers; merging is intersection, and an empty one is a contradiction. Interval arithmetic gives monotone propagators, so a network of them narrows every bound as far as the constraints force. When the constraints are consistent it stops; a contradiction stops only once every empty interval is the same element.\n\nA Sudoku square is not a number to bound but a choice among a few digits, and what we know of it is the set of digits still possible. Next, and last: two capstones.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
